Ribbon Dembow

Ingredients: ONDES MARTENOT + DEMBOW + CONTRABASSOON + PREPARED TUBULAR BELLS + PLANETARIUM FIELD RECORDINGS
Creative Catalyst: A late-night restoration test inside a shuttered planetarium paired an ondes Martenot’s ribbon-controlled glissandi with the clipped low-high engine of dembow. The continuous electronic pitch refused to behave like a synth lead; it bent across the beat like a beam passing through curved glass. A contrabassoonist answered from the projection pit, while prepared tubular bells supplied short metallic attacks cut from what would normally be ceremonial resonance.

Ribbon Dembow is a nocturnal body-music form built around continuous pitch crossing a rigid rhythmic grid. The ondes Martenot is the tonal authority. Ribbon glissandi, keyboard vibrato, diffuse loudspeaker resonance, and slow dynamic swells determine the modal center and the emotional arc. The instrument is not used as retro-futurist decoration. Its ability to move between pitches without discrete steps is the primary compositional device.

Dembow supplies the physical chassis: blunt kick placement, dry snare or clap answers, syncopated low percussion, short repeating cells, and tightly controlled sub-bass. Rather than filling every subdivision, Ribbon Dembow keeps the pattern stripped enough that one ondes gesture can travel across several beats without being obscured. The groove remains bodily and direct, but the melodic layer behaves almost like liquid geometry.

Contrabassoon occupies the dark acoustic floor above the sub. It uses low reed growl, breath-led attacks, key noise, multiphonic pressure, and short chromatic crawls rather than orchestral bass lines. Its purpose is to make the bottom register feel inhabited by air and wood, not only electronics.

Prepared tubular bells provide the upper attack register. Felt collars, removable rubber dampers, soft chain contact, and hand choking transform normally long bell tones into short bronze knocks, partial-rich pings, unstable after-rings, and occasional unresolved metallic blooms. Their pitches are chosen from the same interval field traced by the ondes ribbon.

Planetarium recordings act as architectural memory: projector motors, dome ventilation, seat hinges, star-shutter clicks, lens-carriage movement, and distant public-address test tones. These sounds are never used as cinematic ambience for its own sake. They enter as cues, transients, or rhythmic ghosts that make the room itself part of the meter.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 94–102 BPM, usually 4/4 with a restrained dembow engine, clipped snare answers, low percussion drag, and occasional one-beat voids. The ondes Martenot often glides across barlines, creating long curves against the repeating floor pattern.
- Harmonic Palette: Dorian, Phrygian, minor sixth color, suspended seconds, tritones used sparingly, open fifth pedals, and microtonal approach glides. Chords change slowly; many sections stay on one harmonic field while continuous pitch motion creates apparent modulation.
- Production Signatures:
  1. Ribbon-Orbit Automation: Stereo position follows the ondes ribbon trajectory at reduced depth, so wider glides create subtle lateral motion without turning into obvious panning effects.
  2. Dembow Null Frame: Every few cycles, one expected drum hit is removed and replaced by projector or seat-mechanism noise, preserving pulse through architecture rather than percussion.
  3. Bronze Choke Matrix: Tubular-bell strikes are individually damped to produce several decay classes, from dead knocks to two-second metallic tails, allowing acoustic decay to function like sample-length programming.
  4. Reed-Sub Eclipse: Contrabassoon low notes briefly duck a narrow band of the electronic sub rather than the whole bass bus, making the reed appear to pass in front of the low-frequency field.
  5. Diffuse-Speaker Bloom: Selected ondes phrase endings are sent into a convolution modeled on diffuse resonant loudspeakers and dome reflections, then hard-cut before the next groove cycle.
- Dynamics: Intensity develops through ribbon span, low-reed pressure, and bell-decay length instead of added loudness. Breakdowns often remove the kick but retain a dry clap, projector motor pulse, contrabassoon breath, and one slow ondes glide so the body pulse remains implied.

Scene & Origin Lore
Ribbon Dembow formed during the restoration of a 1970s municipal planetarium in Santo Domingo. The building had been closed for years, but the projection dome, mechanical star projector, steep circular seating, narrow service tunnels, and aging distributed loudspeaker system remained intact.

Ondes Martenot player Amalia Ríos had been invited to test the dome’s acoustics because the instrument could expose pitch smear and uneven reflections more clearly than conventional piano tones. During one session, producer Joel Peña was programming a minimal dembow rhythm through a small monitor system in the control booth. Amalia noticed that long ribbon glissandi stayed intelligible if the drums were short and the sub was carefully narrowed.

Contrabassoonist Tomas Verne joined after hearing one of the restoration tests from the projection pit. Instead of doubling bass notes, he began playing breath-heavy chromatic entries between kick cycles. Percussionist and metalworker Celia Marín completed the quartet by modifying a partial set of tubular bells left from an old educational music program, damping them with removable collars and soft hardware so they could act as precise rhythmic objects.

Their first performances were called orbit checks. Before each piece, the dome projector was rotated to a different azimuth and stopped. That position determined one musical parameter: the starting pitch of the ondes, the number of groove cycles before the contrabassoon entered, or the length of the final bell decay.

The scene’s visual language developed around navy upholstery, brushed aluminum, cream maintenance labels, brass lens fittings, black acoustic fabric, red aisle lights, faded star charts, white work gloves, and old optical cases. The unofficial rule is that no sound may be described as “spacey” unless its motion, decay, or filtering has a specific musical function.

Typical Instruments: Ondes Martenot with ribbon and keyboard, dembow drum programming, restrained mono sub, contrabassoon, prepared tubular bells, dry claps, low hand percussion, projector-motor recordings, dome-ventilation hum, star-shutter clicks, seat hinges, diffuse-speaker convolution, narrow tape echo, filtered noise, and contact microphones on bell frames.

For Fans Of: stripped dembow and experimental reggaetón rhythm, Messiaen-era ondes Martenot color without orchestral excess, Éliane Radigue’s patience, Jlin’s structural precision, and chamber-electronic music where continuous pitch destabilizes a fixed groove.

