Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Azimuth 219 Holds the Groove
- Story & Context: During an orbit check, the restored projector stops at azimuth 219 and refuses to rotate farther. Joel converts the number into the form: two cycles of bare dembow, one cycle with contrabassoon, nine long ondes ribbon sweeps spread across the remainder of the track. Amalia begins on one low Dorian center and draws each glide slightly wider than the previous one. Celia uses only two tubular-bell pitches, damping one immediately and letting the other ring for exactly two seconds.

Tomas enters below the electronic bass but avoids unison, repeatedly approaching the root from a semitone away. Midway through, Joel removes the snare from every fourth cycle and replaces it with the mechanical click of the projector brake. The groove never changes tempo, yet the expanding ribbon range makes the track feel as though the room itself is widening.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Central planetarium projector frozen at azimuth marker 219, ondes Martenot ribbon in the foreground, contrabassoon descending into the projection pit, two prepared tubular bells under red aisle light, faded star chart pinned to a control cabinet. Spoken fragments limited to “two-one-nine,” “hold the brake,” “wider this time,” and “do not move the dome.”

Track 2: Bell Four Dies Before the Star Opens
- Story & Context: Celia discovers that bell four, when wrapped with a narrow felt collar, dies almost completely before the mechanical star shutter finishes opening. The timing becomes the composition’s central relationship. Every bell strike must end before the shutter click, while Amalia’s ondes line begins only after the click has sounded.

Joel builds the sparsest dembow pattern on the EP: kick, clap, one low hand-drum answer, and sub only every second cycle. Tomas plays dry contrabassoon staccatos underneath the bell attacks, then shifts to long breath tones when the ondes enters. In the bridge, Celia removes the felt collar and lets bell four ring freely. Its long decay overlaps the star-shutter mechanism for the first time, breaking the rule. The final section restores the damper and returns the system to strict sequence.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Close-up of tubular bell number 4 wrapped in a narrow felt collar beside a mechanical star-shutter lever, ondes Martenot keys and ribbon edge visible in soft focus, contrabassoon keywork catching red aisle light, brushed aluminum and navy upholstery. Spoken fragments limited to “four ends first,” “wait for the star,” “remove the felt,” and “sequence restored.”

Track 3: The Dome Answers Half a Second Late
- Story & Context: Amalia finds that one ondes tone sent through the old distributed loudspeakers returns from the rear of the dome with a perceptible delayed bloom. Rather than correcting the system, Joel measures the lag and leaves an intentional gap after selected drum cycles so the reflection can answer alone.

Tomas sustains a contrabassoon note through the gap while Celia strikes one partially damped bell exactly when the reflected ondes tone arrives. The resulting three-layer event—reed sustain, delayed electronic pitch, bronze transient—becomes the track’s hook. As the piece develops, Joel gradually removes the architectural gap, forcing the reflection to collide with the groove.

In the last minute, the electronic drums stop. Amalia plays one slow descending ribbon glide into the old loudspeaker network. The ensemble waits through the silence for the dome reflection, and Celia ends the EP with one deadened bell knock after the delayed tone fades.
